    <meta name="dc.description" content="A frequency square is a square matrix in which each row and column is a permutation of the same multiset of symbols. A frequency square is of type $$(n;\lambda )$$ if it contains $$n/\lambda $$ symbols, each of which occurs $$\lambda $$ times per row and $$\lambda $$ times per column. In the case when $$\lambda =n/2$$ we refer to the frequency square as binary. A set of k-MOFS $$(n;\lambda )$$ is a set of k frequency squares of type $$(n;\lambda )$$ such that when any two of the frequency squares are superimposed, each possible ordered pair occurs equally often. A set of k-maxMOFS $$(n;\lambda )$$ is a set of k-MOFS $$(n;\lambda )$$ that is not contained in any set of $$(k+1)$$ -MOFS $$(n;\lambda )$$ . For even n, let $$\mu (n)$$ be the smallest k such that there exists a set of k-maxMOFS(n;&amp;nbsp;n/2). It was shown in Britz et al. (Electron. J. Combin. 27(3):#P3.7, 26 pp, 2020) that $$\mu (n)=1$$ if n/2 is odd and $$\mu (n)&amp;gt;1$$ if n/2 is even. Extending this result, we show that if n/2 is even, then $$\mu (n)&amp;gt;2$$ . Also, we show that whenever n is divisible by a particular function of k, there does not exist a set of $$k&#39;$$ -maxMOFS(n;&amp;nbsp;n/2) for any $$k&#39;\leqslant k$$ . In particular, this means that $$\limsup \mu (n)$$ is unbounded. Nevertheless we can construct infinite families of maximal binary MOFS of fixed cardinality. More generally, let $$q=p^u$$ be a prime power and let $$p^v$$ be the highest power of p that divides n. If $$0\leqslant v-uh"/>
