                    <section aria-labelledby="Abs1" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs1">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs1-content"><p>A <i>frequency square</i> is a square matrix in which each row and column is a permutation of the same multiset of symbols. A frequency square is of <i>type</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\((n;\lambda )\)</span> if it contains <span class="mathjax-tex">\(n/\lambda \)</span> symbols, each of which occurs <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\lambda \)</span> times per row and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\lambda \)</span> times per column. In the case when <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\lambda =n/2\)</span> we refer to the frequency square as <i>binary</i>. A set of <i>k</i>-MOFS<span class="mathjax-tex">\((n;\lambda )\)</span> is a set of <i>k</i> frequency squares of type <span class="mathjax-tex">\((n;\lambda )\)</span> such that when any two of the frequency squares are superimposed, each possible ordered pair occurs equally often. A set of <i>k</i>-maxMOFS<span class="mathjax-tex">\((n;\lambda )\)</span> is a set of <i>k</i>-MOFS<span class="mathjax-tex">\((n;\lambda )\)</span> that is not contained in any set of <span class="mathjax-tex">\((k+1)\)</span>-MOFS<span class="mathjax-tex">\((n;\lambda )\)</span>. For even <i>n</i>, let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mu (n)\)</span> be the smallest <i>k</i> such that there exists a set of <i>k</i>-maxMOFS(<i>n</i>; <i>n</i>/2). It was shown in Britz et al. (Electron. J. Combin. 27(3):#P3.7, 26 pp, 2020) that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mu (n)=1\)</span> if <i>n</i>/2 is odd and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mu (n)&gt;1\)</span> if <i>n</i>/2 is even. Extending this result, we show that if <i>n</i>/2 is even, then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mu (n)&gt;2\)</span>. Also, we show that whenever <i>n</i> is divisible by a particular function of <i>k</i>, there does not exist a set of <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k'\)</span>-maxMOFS(<i>n</i>; <i>n</i>/2) for any <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k'\leqslant k\)</span>. In particular, this means that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\limsup \mu (n)\)</span> is unbounded. Nevertheless we can construct infinite families of maximal binary MOFS of fixed cardinality. More generally, let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(q=p^u\)</span> be a prime power and let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p^v\)</span> be the highest power of <i>p</i> that divides <i>n</i>. If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(0\leqslant v-uh&lt;u/2\)</span> for <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h\geqslant 1\)</span> then we show that there exists a set of <span class="mathjax-tex">\((q^h-1)^2/(q-1)\)</span>-maxMOFS(<i>n</i>; <i>n</i>/<i>q</i>).</p></div></div></section>

                    

                    
    


                    

